  "summary": "Coffins shaped as fish, hens, Bibles and Mercedes cars — sending chiefs of trade off in their own emblem.",
  "description": "Fantasy coffins (abebuu adekai, \"proverb boxes\") began in the 1950s when coffin-maker Seth Kane Kwei built a palanquin-coffin for his grandmother honored as a fisherwoman; the idea caught: cocoa farmers get cocoa-pod coffins, fishermen giant tuna, pilots airplanes, pastors Bibles. Carved from wawa wood, painted in enamel brightness, the coffin celebrates the dead person's trade and status before burial, then enters the ground with them. Museums from Paris to New York now commission remaining workshops for display pieces.",
